   \\]\n   \\[\n   21 = 3 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   28 = 2^2 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute these factorizations into the product:\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 15 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 28 = 3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (2 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (3 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (3 \\cdot 7) \\cdot (2^2 \\cdot 7)\n   \\]\n\n3. Combine all the factors:\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 2^2 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n\n4. Group the prime factors:\n   \\[\n   (3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (7 \\cdot 7)\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify the grouped factors:\n   \\[\n   3^4 \\cdot 2^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2\n   \\]\n\n6. Take the square root of the product:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{3^4 \\cdot 2^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2} = \\sqrt{(3^2)^2 \\cdot (2^2)^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2}\n   \\]\n\n7. Simplify the square root:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{(3^2)^2 \\cdot (2^2)^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2} = 3^2 \\cdot 2^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n\n8. Calculate the final product:\n   \\[\n   3^2 = 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 = 36 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 = 180 \\cdot 7 = 1260\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1260}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1260}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:730"}
